/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Joshua chapters 6-10
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("joshua", [
{n:6, name:"The Fall of Jericho", seal:{ref:"Joshua 6:20",verse:"So the people shouted when the priests blew with the trumpets: and it came to pass, when the people heard the sound of the trumpet, and the people shouted with a great shout, that the wall fell down flat, so that the people went up into the city, every man straight before him, and they took the city."},
 facts:["Did you know Jericho fell to a march, not a battle? No sword was raised against its walls, only seven trumpets and one great shout (Joshua 6:20).","Did you know Rahab, a Gentile woman of Jericho, was the only inhabitant spared, and she later appears in the genealogy of Jesus Christ (Matthew 1:5)?","Did you know Joshua's curse on anyone who rebuilt Jericho was fulfilled centuries later in Hiel the Bethelite, who lost his firstborn and youngest sons when he rebuilt it (1 Kings 16:34)?"],
 questions:[
  {q:"What did the LORD say He had given into Joshua's hand?", options:["Jericho, and the king thereof, and the mighty men of valour","Only the fields outside Jericho","A peace treaty with the king","The road back to Jordan"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 6:2",verse:"And the LORD said unto Joshua, See, I have given into thine hand Jericho, and the king thereof, and the mighty men of valour.", insight:"God announced the victory before a single trumpet blew; He speaks in past tense about promises we have not yet seen."},
  {q:"How often were the men of war to compass the city for six days?", options:["Once a day","Seven times a day","Three times a day","Twice a day"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 6:3",verse:"And ye shall compass the city, all ye men of war, and go round about the city once. Thus shalt thou do six days.", insight:"Once a day for six days: God set the pace, and obedience meant walking a wall down one quiet circuit at a time."},
  {q:"How many priests and trumpets went before the ark?", options:["Seven priests bearing seven trumpets of rams' horns","Twelve priests with silver trumpets","Three priests with one trumpet each","Forty priests with golden trumpets"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 6:4",verse:"And seven priests shall bear before the ark seven trumpets of rams horns: and the seventh day ye shall compass the city seven times, and the priests shall blow with the trumpets.", insight:"Seven priests, seven trumpets, a number of completeness: God was marking this conquest as fully and perfectly His."},
  {q:"What was to happen when the trumpet sounded long?", options:["The people would shout, and the wall would fall down flat","The ark would be lifted over the walls","The priests would enter the city first","The army would set the gates on fire"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 6:5",verse:"And it shall come to pass, that when they make a long blast with the ram’s horn, and when ye hear the sound of the trumpet, all the people shall shout with a great shout; and the wall of the city shall fall down flat, and the people shall ascend up every man straight before him.", insight:"The wall fell at a shout, not a siege engine; God's plans rarely require the tools we would have chosen."},
  {q:"Whom did Joshua call to take up the ark of the LORD?", options:["The priests","The captains of war","The elders of Israel","The Levites of the choir"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 6:6",verse:"And Joshua the son of Nun called the priests, and said unto them, Take up the ark of the covenant, and let seven priests bear seven trumpets of rams’ horns before the ark of the LORD.", insight:"The priests led with the ark; God's presence went first, and the army followed, which is still the right order."},
  {q:"Who went before the priests as the procession marched?", options:["The armed men","The unarmed people","The elders","The children"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 6:8",verse:"And it came to pass, when Joshua had spoken unto the people, that the seven priests bearing the seven trumpets of rams’ horns passed on before the LORD, and blew with the trumpets: and the ark of the covenant of the LORD followed them.", insight:"Armed men guarded the front while priests blew trumpets behind them; worship and warfare marched in the same column."},
  {q:"What came after the ark in the procession?", options:["The rereward","The golden vessels","The elders' tents","The spoil carts"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 6:9",verse:"And the armed men went before the priests that blew with the trumpets, and the rereward came after the ark, the priests going on, and blowing with the trumpets.", insight:"The rereward, the rear guard, closed the column; God protects His people from the front and the back."},
  {q:"What command did Joshua give the people about shouting?", options:["Ye shall not shout, nor make any noise, until the day I bid you shout","Shout as loudly as you can every day","Whisper, but never shout","Shout only at dawn"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 6:10",verse:"And Joshua had commanded the people, saying, Ye shall not shout, nor make any noise with your voice, neither shall any word proceed out of your mouth, until the day I bid you shout; then shall ye shout.", insight:"Six days of silence, then one commanded shout: God teaches His people to wait for His word before raising their voice."},
  {q:"How did the second day's march go?", options:["They compassed the city once and returned into the camp","They compassed it seven times","They attacked the gates","They rested in their tents"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 6:14",verse:"And the second day they compassed the city once, and returned into the camp: so they did six days.", insight:"Day two looked like day one, with nothing to show; faith keeps marching when the miracle is not yet visible."},
  {q:"When did they rise on the seventh day?", options:["Early about the dawning of the day","At noon","After the evening sacrifice","At midnight"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 6:15",verse:"And it came to pass on the seventh day, that they rose early about the dawning of the day, and compassed the city after the same manner seven times: only on that day they compassed the city seven times.", insight:"Early at dawn they began the seventh circling; the greatest victories often start in the quiet of the morning."},
  {q:"How many times did they compass the city on the seventh day?", options:["Seven times","Once","Twelve times","Forty times"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 6:15",verse:"And it came to pass on the seventh day, that they rose early about the dawning of the day, and compassed the city after the same manner seven times: only on that day they compassed the city seven times.", insight:"Seven circuits on the seventh day: God brought the number to fullness before He brought the wall down."},
  {q:"What did Joshua cry at the seventh time?", options:["Shout; for the LORD hath given you the city","Attack, for the gates are open","Flee, for the LORD is angry","Wait, for the walls are strong"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 6:16",verse:"And it came to pass at the seventh time, when the priests blew with the trumpets, Joshua said unto the people, Shout; for the LORD hath given you the city.", insight:"'The LORD hath given you the city' was spoken while the walls still stood; faith shouts the promise before the proof."},
  {q:"Who was to be spared when Jericho fell?", options:["Only Rahab the harlot, and her household","The king and his court","The priests of Baal","Every woman and child"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 6:17",verse:"And the city shall be accursed, even it, and all that are therein, to the LORD: only Rahab the harlot shall live, she and all that are with her in the house, because she hid the messengers that we sent.", insight:"One household was sheltered in a doomed city; God's mercy finds and marks His own even in the middle of judgment."},
  {q:"What warning did Joshua give about the spoils?", options:["Keep yourselves from the accursed thing, lest ye make yourselves accursed","Take whatever you desire for your houses","Burn everything except the gold","Give the spoil to the poor"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 6:18",verse:"And ye, in any wise keep yourselves from the accursed thing, lest ye make yourselves accursed, when ye take of the accursed thing, and make the camp of Israel a curse, and trouble it.", insight:"Greed can turn a victor into a victim; one man's coveting would soon trouble the whole camp of Israel."},
  {q:"Where were the silver, gold, and vessels of brass and iron to go?", options:["Into the treasury of the house of the LORD","Into Joshua's own tent","To the elders for dividing","To be cast into the river"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 6:19",verse:"But all the silver, and gold, and vessels of brass and iron, are consecrated unto the LORD: they shall come into the treasury of the LORD.", insight:"The first spoils of the land belonged to God; the firstfruits still belong to Him, before we touch the rest."},
  {q:"What happened when the people shouted?", options:["The wall fell down flat, and the people went up every man straight before him","The gates swung open on their own","An earthquake swallowed the city","The people of Jericho surrendered"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 6:20",verse:"So the people shouted when the priests blew with the trumpets: and it came to pass, when the people heard the sound of the trumpet, and the people shouted with a great shout, that the wall fell down flat, so that the people went up into the city, every man straight before him, and they took the city.", insight:"The walls did not crumble slowly; they fell flat at a shout, because God, not men, did the breaking."},
  {q:"Who did the two spies bring out of the city?", options:["Rahab, and her father, and her mother, and her brethren","The king of Jericho in chains","The priests with the idols","A hundred children"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 6:22",verse:"But Joshua had said unto the two men that had spied out the country, Go into the harlot’s house, and bring out thence the woman, and all that she hath, as ye sware unto her.", insight:"God keeps His promises down to the last name; not one person under Rahab's roof was left behind."},
  {q:"Where were Rahab's household left after being brought out?", options:["Without the camp of Israel","In the king's palace","In the tabernacle","On Mount Ebal"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 6:23",verse:"And the young men that were spies went in, and brought Rahab, and her father, and her mother, and her brethren, and all that she had; and they brought out all her kindred, and left them without the camp of Israel.", insight:"They were kept outside the camp until they were purified; God welcomes outsiders, but He also makes them clean."},
  {q:"Where does Rahab dwell, according to the end of the chapter?", options:["In Israel even unto this day","In Jericho, rebuilt","In Egypt","In the land of Moab"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 6:25",verse:"And Joshua saved Rahab the harlot alive, and her father’s household, and all that she had; and she dwelleth in Israel even unto this day; because she hid the messengers, which Joshua sent to spy out Jericho.", insight:"The woman of Jericho became a daughter of Israel; grace rewrites the address of anyone who trusts God."},
  {q:"What curse did Joshua pronounce on rebuilding Jericho?", options:["He shall lay the foundation thereof in his firstborn, and set up the gates in his youngest son","The city shall sink into the Jordan","Fire shall fall from heaven on its builders","Its builders shall wander forty years"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 6:26",verse:"And Joshua adjured them at that time, saying, Cursed be the man before the LORD, that riseth up and buildeth this city Jericho: he shall lay the foundation thereof in his firstborn, and in his youngest son shall he set up the gates of it.", insight:"Joshua named the exact price of rebuilding what God tore down; some things God finishes are meant to stay finished."}
 ]},
{n:7, name:"Achan's Sin and the Defeat at Ai", seal:{ref:"Joshua 7:26",verse:"And they raised over him a great heap of stones unto this day. So the LORD turned from the fierceness of his anger. Wherefore the name of that place was called, The valley of Achor, unto this day."},
 facts:["Did you know Achan's stolen goods were a Babylonish garment, two hundred shekels of silver, and a fifty-shekel wedge of gold, all buried under his tent (Joshua 7:21)?","Did you know the defeat at Ai cost Israel only thirty-six men, yet their hearts melted because it was their first loss in the land (Joshua 7:5)?","Did you know the valley of Achan's judgment was named Achor, meaning trouble, and later Scripture turns it into a door of hope (Hosea 2:15)?"],
 questions:[
  {q:"Who took of the accursed thing and brought God's anger on Israel?", options:["Achan, the son of Carmi, of the tribe of Judah","Korah, the son of Izhar","Dathan of the tribe of Reuben","Zimri the Simeonite"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 7:1",verse:"But the children of Israel committed a trespass in the accursed thing: for Achan, the son of Carmi, the son of Zabdi, the son of Zerah, of the tribe of Judah, took of the accursed thing: and the anger of the LORD was kindled against the children of Israel.", insight:"One man's hidden sin brought anger on the whole nation; private disobedience is never truly private."},
  {q:"Where did Joshua send men to spy out the land?", options:["To Ai, which is beside Bethaven, on the east side of Bethel","To Hazor in the north","To Hebron in the hills","To the plains of Moab"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 7:2",verse:"And Joshua sent men from Jericho to Ai, which is beside Beth-aven, on the east side of Bethel, and spake unto them, saying, Go up and view the country. And the men went up and viewed Ai.", insight:"They scouted Ai the way they had scouted Jericho, but Jericho's victory had come from God's presence, not from scouting."},
  {q:"What did the spies advise about attacking Ai?", options:["Let about two or three thousand men go up, for they are but few","Send the whole army of six hundred thousand","Wait until the walls crumble","Attack at night with torches"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 7:3",verse:"And they returned to Joshua, and said unto him, Let not all the people go up; but let about two or three thousand men go up and smite Ai; and make not all the people to labour thither; for they are but few.", insight:"They sized up Ai with human eyes and found it small; they had forgotten to ask whether God was with them."},
  {q:"How many men went up to Ai, and what happened?", options:["About three thousand men, and they fled before the men of Ai","Ten thousand, and they took the city","Forty thousand, and they besieged it","Five hundred, and they were captured"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 7:4",verse:"So there went up thither of the people about three thousand men: and they fled before the men of Ai.", insight:"Three thousand fled where Jericho had fallen before a shout; numbers mean nothing when God is not in the ranks."},
  {q:"How many men of Israel did the men of Ai smite?", options:["About thirty and six men","Three hundred men","Three thousand men","Thirty thousand men"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 7:5",verse:"And the men of Ai smote of them about thirty and six men: for they chased them from before the gate even unto Shebarim, and smote them in the going down: wherefore the hearts of the people melted, and became as water.", insight:"Thirty-six lives, a small number beside Jericho's thousands, yet the hearts of the people melted; defeat after victory stings deepest."},
  {q:"What did Joshua do when he heard of the defeat?", options:["He rent his clothes, and fell to the earth upon his face before the ark","He ordered a second attack at once","He blamed the spies before the people","He burned the camp"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 7:6",verse:"And Joshua rent his clothes, and fell to the earth upon his face before the ark of the LORD until the eventide, he and the elders of Israel, and put dust upon their heads.", insight:"Joshua tore his clothes and fell on his face; the strongest leaders are the first to their knees when things go wrong."},
  {q:"What did Joshua wish in his distress?", options:["Would to God we had been content, and dwelt on the other side Jordan","That Moses were still alive to lead them","That they had never left Egypt","That the walls of Jericho stood again"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 7:7",verse:"And Joshua said, Alas, O Lord GOD, wherefore hast thou at all brought this people over Jordan, to deliver us into the hand of the Amorites, to destroy us? would to God we had been content, and dwelt on the other side Jordan!", insight:"In despair Joshua wished they had stayed across the Jordan; grief talks loudly, but it does not always talk wisely."},
  {q:"What did Joshua fear the Canaanites would do to God's name?", options:["What wilt thou do unto thy great name?","They will worship it","They will forget it","They will carve it in stone"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 7:9",verse:"For the Canaanites and all the inhabitants of the land shall hear of it, and shall environ us round, and cut off our name from the earth: and what wilt thou do unto thy great name?", insight:"Joshua's deepest plea was for God's own name; when you pray for His glory more than your comfort, you pray rightly."},
  {q:"What did the LORD command Joshua to do?", options:["Get thee up; wherefore liest thou thus upon thy face?","Lie still until morning","Go back across Jordan","Mourn for seven days"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 7:10",verse:"And the LORD said unto Joshua, Get thee up; wherefore liest thou thus upon thy face?", insight:"God told Joshua to get up; there is a time to pray on your face and a time to stand and deal with the sin."},
  {q:"What was Israel's real problem, according to the LORD?", options:["Israel hath sinned, and taken of the accursed thing","Israel had too few soldiers","Israel had marched too far","Israel had forgotten their weapons"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 7:11",verse:"Israel hath sinned, and they have also transgressed my covenant which I commanded them: for they have even taken of the accursed thing, and have also stolen, and dissembled also, and they have put it even among their own stuff.", insight:"The defeat was not a military problem but a moral one; God named it plainly: sin, theft, and deceit in the camp."},
  {q:"What did God say would happen unless the accursed thing was destroyed?", options:["Neither will I be with you any more","You shall wander forty more years","The Jordan shall flood again","Your children shall be slaves"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 7:12",verse:"Therefore the children of Israel could not stand before their enemies, but turned their backs before their enemies, because they were accursed: neither will I be with you any more, except ye destroy the accursed from among you.", insight:"God's presence was the condition of their victory; tolerated sin costs more than any battle ever could."},
  {q:"What were the people commanded to do against the morrow?", options:["Sanctify yourselves","Build a new altar","Count the fighting men","March around Ai"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 7:13",verse:"Up, sanctify the people, and say, Sanctify yourselves against to morrow: for thus saith the LORD God of Israel, There is an accursed thing in the midst of thee, O Israel: thou canst not stand before thine enemies, until ye take away the accursed thing from among you.", insight:"Sanctify yourselves: before God would fight for them again, they had to cleanse what was among them."},
  {q:"How was the guilty one to be found?", options:["Brought by tribes, by families, by households, man by man","By casting lots among the captains only","By the testimony of two witnesses","By searching every tent at once"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 7:14",verse:"In the morning therefore ye shall be brought according to your tribes: and it shall be, that the tribe which the LORD taketh shall come according to the families thereof; and the family which the LORD shall take shall come by households; and the household which the LORD shall take shall come man by man.", insight:"Tribe by tribe, family by family, man by man: no one can hide from God, and His net draws steadily closer."},
  {q:"What was to be done with the one taken with the accursed thing?", options:["He shall be burnt with fire, he and all that he hath","He shall be exiled across Jordan","He shall pay double the value","He shall serve as a slave"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 7:15",verse:"And it shall be, that he that is taken with the accursed thing shall be burnt with fire, he and all that he hath: because he hath transgressed the covenant of the LORD, and because he hath wrought folly in Israel.", insight:"The judgment was severe because the sin had endangered the nation; God treats covenant-breaking with covenant seriousness."},
  {q:"When did Joshua begin the search?", options:["He rose up early in the morning, and brought Israel by their tribes","At midnight with torches","After the evening sacrifice","On the seventh day"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 7:16",verse:"So Joshua rose up early in the morning, and brought Israel by their tribes; and the tribe of Judah was taken:", insight:"Joshua rose early to the hard task; dealing with sin is morning work, not something to put off until evening."},
  {q:"Who was finally taken as the guilty man?", options:["Achan, the son of Carmi, of the tribe of Judah","Zabdi the father","Zerah the grandfather","A captain of Judah"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 7:18",verse:"And he brought his household man by man; and Achan, the son of Carmi, the son of Zabdi, the son of Zerah, of the tribe of Judah, was taken.", insight:"The search that began with all Israel ended at one man's tent; hidden sin is always personal before it is public."},
  {q:"How did Joshua address Achan before asking for confession?", options:["My son, give, I pray thee, glory to the LORD God of Israel","Thou son of wickedness","Thief of the camp","Enemy of Israel"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 7:19",verse:"And Joshua said unto Achan, My son, give, I pray thee, glory to the LORD God of Israel, and make confession unto him; and tell me now what thou hast done; hide it not from me.", insight:"'My son' even at the moment of exposure; Joshua spoke with a father's tenderness while doing a judge's duty."},
  {q:"What had Achan taken and hidden?", options:["A goodly Babylonish garment, two hundred shekels of silver, and a wedge of gold of fifty shekels","The crown of the king of Jericho","Silver trumpets from the priests","The golden calf's ornaments"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 7:21",verse:"When I saw among the spoils a goodly Babylonish garment, and two hundred shekels of silver, and a wedge of gold of fifty shekels weight, then I coveted them, and took them; and, behold, they are hid in the earth in the midst of my tent, and the silver under it.", insight:"A garment, some silver, a wedge of gold: the price of a man's life and a nation's defeat was smaller than he dreamed."},
  {q:"Where were the stolen things found?", options:["Hid in the earth in the midst of his tent","Buried under the altar","In a cave near Ai","Cast into the Jordan"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 7:22",verse:"So Joshua sent messengers, and they ran unto the tent; and, behold, it was hid in his tent, and the silver under it.", insight:"Buried in his own tent, the sin he thought was hidden; what we bury, God digs up, and messengers run to see it."},
  {q:"What did Joshua say as Achan was judged?", options:["Why hast thou troubled us? the LORD shall trouble thee this day","The LORD forgive thee","Thy children shall go free","Take thy goods and depart"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 7:25",verse:"And Joshua said, Why hast thou troubled us? the LORD shall trouble thee this day. And all Israel stoned him with stones, and burned them with fire, after they had stoned them with stones.", insight:"'Why hast thou troubled us?' One man's trouble became everyone's trouble; that is the arithmetic of sin in a covenant people."}
 ]},
{n:8, name:"The Fall of Ai", seal:{ref:"Joshua 8:34",verse:"And afterward he read all the words of the law, the blessings and cursings, according to all that is written in the book of the law."},
 facts:["Did you know Israel was allowed to keep Ai's spoil and cattle for themselves, unlike at Jericho, a reward after the discipline of Achan (Joshua 8:2)?","Did you know Joshua copied the whole law of Moses onto stones at Mount Ebal, setting the covenant literally in stone before the battles resumed (Joshua 8:32)?","Did you know half of Israel stood on Mount Gerizim and half on Mount Ebal while Joshua read the blessings and the cursings aloud (Joshua 8:33)?"],
 questions:[
  {q:"What did the LORD say to Joshua after the defeat at Ai?", options:["Fear not, neither be thou dismayed: I have given into thy hand the king of Ai","Return across Jordan","Make peace with Ai","Wait seven years"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 8:1",verse:"And the LORD said unto Joshua, Fear not, neither be thou dismayed: take all the people of war with thee, and arise, go up to Ai: see, I have given into thy hand the king of Ai, and his people, and his city, and his land:", insight:"'Fear not' came right after their worst defeat; God's comfort is not reserved for the days when we have not failed."},
  {q:"How was Ai to differ from Jericho in its spoil?", options:["Only the spoil thereof, and the cattle thereof, shall ye take for a prey unto yourselves","Nothing was to be taken","The spoil was to go to the priests","Half the spoil was to be burned"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 8:2",verse:"And thou shalt do to Ai and her king as thou didst unto Jericho and her king: only the spoil thereof, and the cattle thereof, shall ye take for a prey unto yourselves: lay thee an ambush for the city behind it.", insight:"At Jericho all was God's; at Ai the spoil was theirs. Obedience after discipline opens doors that disobedience had shut."},
  {q:"How many mighty men of valour did Joshua send by night?", options:["Thirty thousand","Three thousand","Three hundred","Three hundred thousand"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 8:3",verse:"So Joshua arose, and all the people of war, to go up against Ai: and Joshua chose out thirty thousand mighty men of valour, and sent them away by night.", insight:"Thirty thousand by night, where three thousand had fled by day; the difference was not the numbers but the command of God."},
  {q:"What was the ambush commanded to do?", options:["Lie in wait against the city, even behind the city","Attack the gates at dawn","Dig a trench around Ai","Set the fields on fire"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 8:4",verse:"And he commanded them, saying, Behold, ye shall lie in wait against the city, even behind the city: go not very far from the city, but be ye all ready:", insight:"The victory would come from men nobody could see; God often wins His battles with the troops we never notice."},
  {q:"What did Joshua expect the men of Ai to say when Israel fled?", options:["They flee before us, as at the first","The LORD has forsaken them","They fear our king","They return to Jordan"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 8:6",verse:"(For they will come out after us) till we have drawn them from the city; for they will say, They flee before us, as at the first: therefore we will flee before them.", insight:"Ai would read the retreat as weakness, exactly as Joshua planned; God lets our enemies misread us when it serves His purpose."},
  {q:"What was the ambush to do once it had taken the city?", options:["Set the city on fire","Open the gates for the army","Spare the houses of the poor","Sound the trumpets"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 8:8",verse:"And it shall be, when ye have taken the city, that ye shall set the city on fire: according to the commandment of the LORD shall ye do. See, I have commanded you.", insight:"Fire was the signal of a finished conquest; when God gives the victory, He means it to be complete, not partial."},
  {q:"What did Joshua do early in the morning?", options:["He rose up early in the morning, and numbered the people","He offered a burnt offering","He sent the spies again","He wrote the law on stones"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 8:10",verse:"And Joshua rose up early in the morning, and numbered the people, and went up, he and the elders of Israel, before the people to Ai.", insight:"He numbered the people at dawn; careful preparation is not a lack of faith, it is the shape faith takes before battle."},
  {q:"On which side of Ai did the main host pitch?", options:["On the north side of Ai, with a valley between them and Ai","On the south, by the river","On the east, at the gate","On the west, in the hills"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 8:11",verse:"And all the people, even the people of war that were with him, went up, and drew nigh, and came before the city, and pitched on the north side of Ai: now there was a valley between them and Ai.", insight:"North of Ai, across a valley: God arranged the ground itself, and even the geography served His ambush."},
  {q:"How many men did Joshua set in ambush between Bethel and Ai?", options:["Five thousand men","Five hundred men","Fifty thousand men","Fifty men"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 8:12",verse:"And he took about five thousand men, and set them to lie in ambush between Bethel and Ai, on the west side of the city.", insight:"Five thousand hidden on the west side, a second net behind the first; God layers His plans deeper than we can see."},
  {q:"How did Joshua and all Israel pretend before the men of Ai?", options:["They made as if they were beaten before them, and fled by the way of the wilderness","They marched in silence","They sounded a retreat at noon","They hid in the valley"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 8:15",verse:"And Joshua and all Israel made as if they were beaten before them, and fled by the way of the wilderness.", insight:"They acted beaten to draw the enemy out; sometimes God asks us to look weak so that He can show Himself strong."},
  {q:"What happened in Ai and Bethel when the pursuit began?", options:["There was not a man left in Ai or Bethel, that went not out after Israel","The elders shut the gates","Half the men stayed to guard the spoil","The king fled to Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 8:17",verse:"And there was not a man left in Ai or Bethel, that went not out after Israel: and they left the city open, and pursued after Israel.", insight:"Every last man chased the fleeing Israelites; overconfidence empties the fortress before the real attack begins."},
  {q:"What did the LORD tell Joshua to stretch out toward Ai?", options:["The spear that is in thy hand","His staff","The ark of the covenant","A banner of blue"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 8:18",verse:"And the LORD said unto Joshua, Stretch out the spear that is in thy hand toward Ai; for I will give it into thine hand. And Joshua stretched out the spear that he had in his hand toward the city.", insight:"A stretched-out spear was the sign; God gives His servants visible tokens that His invisible power is at work."},
  {q:"What did the ambush do when the spear was stretched out?", options:["They arose quickly out of their place, entered the city, and set it on fire","They sounded the trumpets","They marched on the gates","They called to the main host"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 8:19",verse:"And the ambush arose quickly out of their place, and they ran as soon as he had stretched out his hand: and they entered into the city, and took it, and hasted and set the city on fire.", insight:"They arose quickly; when God gives the signal, the obedient do not stroll, they run."},
  {q:"What did Joshua and Israel do when they saw the smoke of the city?", options:["They turned again, and slew the men of Ai","They fled further into the wilderness","They waited until nightfall","They sent for the ark"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 8:21",verse:"And when Joshua and all Israel saw that the ambush had taken the city, and that the smoke of the city ascended, then they turned again, and slew the men of Ai.", insight:"The fleeing became the pursuers in a moment; God can turn our retreats into routs when the time is right."},
  {q:"How many fell that day, all the men of Ai?", options:["Twelve thousand","Twelve hundred","Seventy thousand","Thirty-six"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 8:25",verse:"And so it was, that all that fell that day, both of men and women, were twelve thousand, even all the men of Ai.", insight:"Twelve thousand fell; the city that had humbled Israel with thirty-six deaths was now utterly broken by God's hand."},
  {q:"How long did Joshua keep his spear stretched out?", options:["Until he had utterly destroyed all the inhabitants of Ai","Until sunset","Until the ambush returned","Until the king surrendered"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 8:26",verse:"For Joshua drew not his hand back, wherewith he stretched out the spear, until he had utterly destroyed all the inhabitants of Ai.", insight:"He did not draw his hand back until the work was done; perseverance finishes what obedience begins."},
  {q:"What was done with the king of Ai?", options:["He was hanged on a tree until eventide, then buried under a heap of stones","He was made a servant in Gilgal","He was sent to Egypt in chains","He was pardoned and released"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 8:29",verse:"And the king of Ai he hanged on a tree until eventide: and as soon as the sun was down, Joshua commanded that they should take his carcase down from the tree, and cast it at the entering of the gate of the city, and raise thereon a great heap of stones, that remaineth unto this day.", insight:"The king who had mocked Israel hung on a tree till evening; pride's last address is lower than its first."},
  {q:"What did Joshua build in Mount Ebal?", options:["An altar unto the LORD God of Israel","A tower of stones","A house for the ark","A monument to the victory"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 8:30",verse:"Then Joshua built an altar unto the LORD God of Israel in mount Ebal,", insight:"After the sword came the altar; victory is not complete until it is turned into worship."},
  {q:"What did Joshua write upon the stones?", options:["A copy of the law of Moses","The names of the twelve tribes","The story of the crossing of Jordan","The curses on Jericho"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 8:32",verse:"And he wrote there upon the stones a copy of the law of Moses, which he wrote in the presence of the children of Israel.", insight:"He copied the law onto stones in the land; God's word was to be as permanent in Canaan as the rocks themselves."},
  {q:"What did Joshua read before all the congregation of Israel?", options:["All the words of the law, the blessings and cursings","Only the blessings","The genealogies of the tribes","The song of Moses"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 8:34",verse:"And afterward he read all the words of the law, the blessings and cursings, according to all that is written in the book of the law.", insight:"Blessings and cursings, nothing left out; a faithful teacher reads the whole counsel of God, not just the comfortable parts."}
 ]},
{n:9, name:"The Gibeonites' Deception", seal:{ref:"Joshua 9:14",verse:"And the men took of their victuals, and asked not counsel at the mouth of the LORD."},
 facts:["Did you know Israel's great mistake at Gibeon was tasting the strangers' bread but never asking the LORD's counsel before swearing the oath (Joshua 9:14)?","Did you know Gibeon was no village but a great royal city, greater than Ai, which is why its surrender terrified the neighboring kings (Joshua 10:2)?","Did you know the Gibeonites' punishment became their service: they became hewers of wood and drawers of water for the congregation and the altar, a post they still held when the record was written (Joshua 9:27)?"],
 questions:[
  {q:"Who heard what Israel had done and prepared to resist?", options:["All the kings on this side Jordan, in the hills, and in the valleys","Only the king of Jericho","The Philistines of the coast","The kings beyond the Euphrates"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 9:1",verse:"And it came to pass, when all the kings which were on this side Jordan, in the hills, and in the valleys, and in all the coasts of the great sea over against Lebanon, the Hittite, and the Amorite, the Canaanite, the Perizzite, the Hivite, and the Jebusite, heard thereof;", insight:"News of Jericho and Ai traveled fast; a people God fights for becomes famous, and fame draws both fear and schemes."},
  {q:"How did the kings plan to meet Joshua?", options:["They gathered themselves together, to fight with Joshua, with one accord","They sent ambassadors for peace","They fled across the Jordan","They hid in the caves"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 9:2",verse:"That they gathered themselves together, to fight with Joshua and with Israel, with one accord.", insight:"With one accord they united against Israel; shared fear makes strange allies, but it cannot outvote God."},
  {q:"Who heard what Joshua had done unto Jericho and to Ai and chose a different path?", options:["The inhabitants of Gibeon","The men of Hebron","The Jebusites","The Hivites of Shechem"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 9:3",verse:"And when the inhabitants of Gibeon heard what Joshua had done unto Jericho and to Ai,", insight:"Gibeon heard the same news as the other kings but chose cunning over courage; fear can be clever and still be wrong."},
  {q:"What did the Gibeonites do wilily?", options:["They made as if they had been ambassadors from a far country","They bribed the elders with gold","They poisoned the wells","They burned their own city"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 9:4",verse:"They did work wilily, and went and made as if they had been ambassadors, and took old sacks upon their asses, and wine bottles, old, and rent, and bound up;", insight:"'They did work wilily': Scripture names their craft for what it was; cleverness that deceives God's people is still deceit."},
  {q:"How did they disguise their bread?", options:["It was dry and mouldy","It was baked in the shape of idols","It was mixed with honey","It was still steaming hot"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 9:5",verse:"And old shoes and clouted upon their feet, and old garments upon them; and all the bread of their provision was dry and mouldy.", insight:"Mouldy bread was their best evidence; lies are often dressed in the costume of poverty and hardship."},
  {q:"Where did the Gibeonites go to meet Joshua?", options:["Unto the camp at Gilgal","Unto the plains of Jericho","Unto Mount Ebal","Unto the ford of Jordan"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 9:6",verse:"And they went to Joshua unto the camp at Gilgal, and said unto him, and to the men of Israel, We be come from a far country: now therefore make ye a league with us.", insight:"They came to Gilgal, the camp of covenant and circumcision, carrying a lie into the place of truth."},
  {q:"What suspicious question did the men of Israel ask?", options:["Peradventure ye dwell among us; and how shall we make a league with you?","Are you armed for war?","Who is your king?","What gods do you serve?"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 9:7",verse:"And the men of Israel said unto the Hivites, Peradventure ye dwell among us; and how shall we make a league with you?", insight:"Israel asked the right question and then ignored its own warning; discernment that is not obeyed is only decoration."},
  {q:"What did the Gibeonites call themselves before Joshua?", options:["Thy servants","Thy brethren","Thy equals","Thy masters"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 9:8",verse:"And they said unto Joshua, We are thy servants. And Joshua said unto them, Who are ye? and from whence come ye?", insight:"'We are thy servants': flattery opens doors that honesty could not; beware the stranger who bows too low, too fast."},
  {q:"Why did they claim to have come from a very far country?", options:["Because of the name of the LORD thy God: for we have heard the fame of him","To trade in purple and fine linen","To learn the art of war","To escape a famine"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 9:9",verse:"And they said unto him, From a very far country thy servants are come because of the name of the LORD thy God: for we have heard the fame of him, and all that he did in Egypt,", insight:"They used God's fame as their cover story; even true words about God can be twisted into a lie's clothing."},
  {q:"Which victories did they say they had heard of?", options:["What he did unto Sihon and Og, the two kings of the Amorites","The fall of Jericho","The crossing of the Red Sea","The defeat of Pharaoh"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 9:10",verse:"And all that he did to the two kings of the Amorites, that were beyond Jordan, to Sihon king of Heshbon, and to Og king of Bashan, which was at Ashtaroth.", insight:"They named Sihon and Og but never Jericho or Ai; liars edit the story, leaving out the parts that would expose them."},
  {q:"What had their elders told them to say?", options:["We are your servants: therefore now make ye a league with us","Give us your daughters in marriage","Teach us your law","Let us dwell in your tents"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 9:11",verse:"Wherefore our elders and all the inhabitants of our country spake to us, saying, Take victuals with you for the journey, and go to meet them, and say unto them, We are your servants: therefore now make ye a league with us.", insight:"Their elders scripted every word; a lie rehearsed by committee is still a lie, however polished the performance."},
  {q:"What did they say about their bread?", options:["This our bread we took hot for our provision out of our houses on the day we came forth","It was baked by our priests","It never grows stale","It was a gift from your God"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 9:12",verse:"This our bread we took hot for our provision out of our houses on the day we came forth to go unto you; but now, behold, it is dry, and it is mouldy:", insight:"Hot bread turned mouldy was their proof of a long journey; the best lies always bring props."},
  {q:"What did they say about their wine bottles, garments, and shoes?", options:["They are become old by reason of the very long journey","They were spoiled in battle","They were borrowed from travelers","They were cursed by their gods"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 9:13",verse:"And these bottles of wine, which we filled, were new; and, behold, they be rent: and these our garments and our shoes are become old by reason of the very long journey.", insight:"Rent bottles and clouted shoes told their tale; appearances can be arranged, which is why we must look deeper."},
  {q:"What was Israel's fatal mistake?", options:["The men took of their victuals, and asked not counsel at the mouth of the LORD","They refused the strangers bread","They attacked the ambassadors","They demanded tribute"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 9:14",verse:"And the men took of their victuals, and asked not counsel at the mouth of the LORD.", insight:"They tasted the bread but never asked the LORD; the senses are poor counselors when God has not been consulted."},
  {q:"What did Joshua do with the Gibeonites?", options:["He made peace with them, and made a league with them, to let them live","He drove them from the camp","He made them pay tribute in gold","He sent them back to Gibeon"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 9:15",verse:"And Joshua made peace with them, and made a league with them, to let them live: and the princes of the congregation sware unto them.", insight:"Joshua made peace in haste and the princes swore to it; a rushed oath still binds, which is why oaths should never be rushed."},
  {q:"When did Israel discover the truth?", options:["At the end of three days, that they were their neighbours","After forty days","When the Gibeonites attacked","Never, until the next generation"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 9:16",verse:"And it came to pass at the end of three days after they had made a league with them, that they heard that they were their neighbours, and that they dwelt among them.", insight:"Three days: deception has a short shelf life. Truth walks in on schedule, even when we have signed without it."},
  {q:"Which cities belonged to the Gibeonites?", options:["Gibeon, and Chephirah, and Beeroth, and Kirjath-jearim","Jericho and Ai","Hebron and Debir","Lachish and Eglon"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 9:17",verse:"And the children of Israel journeyed, and came unto their cities on the third day. Now their cities were Gibeon, and Chephirah, and Beeroth, and Kirjath-jearim.", insight:"Four cities, not one wandering band; the 'far country' was next door all along, and the lie was bigger than it looked."},
  {q:"Why did Israel not smite the Gibeonites when the trick was known?", options:["Because the princes of the congregation had sworn unto them by the LORD God of Israel","Because they feared their army","Because Joshua commanded mercy","Because the Gibeonites paid ransom"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 9:18",verse:"And the children of Israel smote them not, because the princes of the congregation had sworn unto them by the LORD God of Israel. And all the congregation murmured against the princes.", insight:"An oath sworn in God's name held even when it was tricked out of them; God's people keep their word, even at a cost."},
  {q:"What service was appointed for the Gibeonites?", options:["Let them be hewers of wood and drawers of water for all the congregation","Let them guard the borders","Let them tend the flocks","Let them build the walls"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 9:21",verse:"And the princes said unto them, Let them live; but let them be hewers of wood and drawers of water unto all the congregation; as the princes had promised them.", insight:"Hewers of wood and drawers of water: their deceit earned them a lifetime of humble service at the altar they had tried to fool."},
  {q:"What curse did Joshua pronounce on them?", options:["None of you shall be freed from being bondmen, and hewers of wood and drawers of water","You shall be driven from the land","Your cities shall be burned","Your children shall be slaves in Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 9:23",verse:"Now therefore ye are cursed, and there shall none of you be freed from being bondmen, and hewers of wood and drawers of water for the house of my God.", insight:"The curse became a calling; centuries later their descendants still served at God's house, for God redeems even judged people."}
 ]},
{n:10, name:"The Sun Stands Still", seal:{ref:"Joshua 10:13",verse:"And the sun stood still, and the moon stayed, until the people had avenged themselves upon their enemies. Is not this written in the book of Jasher? So the sun stood still in the midst of heaven, and hasted not to go down about a whole day."},
 facts:["Did you know more of Israel's enemies died from the great hailstones the LORD cast down than from the swords of Israel (Joshua 10:11)?","Did you know Joshua commanded the sun to stand still over Gibeon and the moon over the valley of Ajalon, and Scripture says no day before or after was like it (Joshua 10:14)?","Did you know the five defeated kings were hanged on five trees until evening, then cast into the very cave where they had hidden, with great stones laid at its mouth (Joshua 10:26, 27)?"],
 questions:[
  {q:"Which king heard how Joshua had taken Ai and that Gibeon had made peace?", options:["Adonizedek king of Jerusalem","The king of Hazor","The king of Gezer","The king of Megiddo"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 10:1",verse:"Now it came to pass, when Adoni-zedek king of Jerusalem had heard how Joshua had taken Ai, and had utterly destroyed it; as he had done to Jericho and her king, so he had done to Ai and her king; and how the inhabitants of Gibeon had made peace with Israel, and were among them;", insight:"Adonizedek heard the news and organized a coalition; every victory of God's people stirs the enemy to new counsels."},
  {q:"Why did Adonizedek fear greatly?", options:["Because Gibeon was a great city, as one of the royal cities, and greater than Ai","Because his army had deserted","Because of a famine in the land","Because of a plague in Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 10:2",verse:"That they feared greatly, because Gibeon was a great city, as one of the royal cities, and because it was greater than Ai, and all the men thereof were mighty.", insight:"A great city greater than Ai had surrendered without a fight; when God is clearly at work, even kings lose their nerve."},
  {q:"To which four kings did Adonizedek send?", options:["The kings of Hebron, Jarmuth, Lachish, and Eglon","The kings of Hazor, Madon, Shimron, and Achshaph","The kings of Jericho, Ai, Bethel, and Shechem","The kings of Gezer, Debir, Hormah, and Arad"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 10:3",verse:"Wherefore Adoni-zedek king of Jerusalem sent unto Hoham king of Hebron, and unto Piram king of Jarmuth, and unto Japhia king of Lachish, and unto Debir king of Eglon, saying,", insight:"Four kings answered one king's letter; fear is a fast courier, but it delivers its runners straight into God's trap."},
  {q:"What did Adonizedek ask the kings to do?", options:["Come up unto me, and help me, that we may smite Gibeon","Send tribute to Jerusalem","Guard the Jordan fords","Attack Gilgal by night"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 10:4",verse:"Come up unto me, and help me, that we may smite Gibeon: for it hath made peace with Joshua and with the children of Israel.", insight:"'Smite Gibeon, for it hath made peace': the world still punishes those who make peace with God's people."},
  {q:"How many kings of the Amorites came against Gibeon?", options:["Five kings","Three kings","Seven kings","Twelve kings"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 10:5",verse:"Therefore the five kings of the Amorites, the king of Jerusalem, the king of Hebron, the king of Jarmuth, the king of Lachish, the king of Eglon, gathered themselves together, and went up, they and all their hosts, and encamped before Gibeon, and made war against it.", insight:"Five kings, five armies, one doomed alliance; when God fights for Israel, the odds are always in His favor."},
  {q:"What message did the men of Gibeon send to Joshua at Gilgal?", options:["Slack not thy hand from thy servants; come up to us quickly, and save us","Make peace with the five kings","Send us weapons and chariots","Flee, for the kings are too many"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 10:6",verse:"And the men of Gibeon sent unto Joshua to the camp to Gilgal, saying, Slack not thy hand from thy servants; come up to us quickly, and save us, and help us: for all the kings of the Amorites that dwell in the mountains are gathered together against us.", insight:"Gibeon cried to the very people it had deceived, and Joshua came; covenant mercy answers even the unworthy who call."},
  {q:"Who went up from Gilgal to save Gibeon?", options:["Joshua, and all the people of war, and all the mighty men of valour","Joshua alone with his staff","The priests with the ark only","Two thousand chosen men"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 10:7",verse:"So Joshua ascended from Gilgal, he, and all the people of war with him, and all the mighty men of valour.", insight:"All the people of war went up; when God gives the word, half measures have no place in obedience."},
  {q:"What promise did the LORD give Joshua before the battle?", options:["Fear them not: for I have delivered them into thine hand","Flee before their chariots","Wait until the new moon","Send the ark first"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 10:8",verse:"And the LORD said unto Joshua, Fear them not: for I have delivered them into thine hand; there shall not a man of them stand before thee.", insight:"'I have delivered them' was spoken before a sword was drawn; God hands us victories in promise before He hands them in battle."},
  {q:"How did Joshua reach the enemy?", options:["He came unto them suddenly, and went up from Gilgal all night","He marched by day with banners","He sailed up the Jordan","He waited seven days"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 10:9",verse:"Joshua therefore came unto them suddenly, and went up from Gilgal all night.", insight:"All night he marched to arrive suddenly; God honors the leader who spends the night climbing toward obedience."},
  {q:"What did the LORD do to the Amorites at Gibeon?", options:["He discomfited them before Israel, and slew them with a great slaughter","He sent a plague upon their camp","He dried up their wells","He turned their chariots against them"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 10:10",verse:"And the LORD discomfited them before Israel, and slew them with a great slaughter at Gibeon, and chased them along the way that goeth up to Beth-horon, and smote them to Azekah, and unto Makkedah.", insight:"The LORD discomfited them; panic in the enemy's ranks is one of God's favorite weapons, and He wields it Himself."},
  {q:"What fell from heaven upon the fleeing Amorites?", options:["Great stones from heaven, hailstones","Fire and brimstone","A flood of water","Darkness"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 10:11",verse:"And it came to pass, as they fled from before Israel, and were in the going down to Beth-horon, that the LORD cast down great stones from heaven upon them unto Azekah, and they died: they were more which died with hailstones than they whom the children of Israel slew with the sword.", insight:"Heaven threw the stones while Israel chased; when God's people fight His battles, creation itself enlists."},
  {q:"What did Joshua say to the sun and the moon?", options:["Sun, stand thou still upon Gibeon; and thou, Moon, in the valley of Ajalon","Sun, hide thy face from the wicked","Moon, turn thy light to blood","Sun, hasten thy setting"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 10:12",verse:"Then spake Joshua to the LORD in the day when the LORD delivered up the Amorites before the children of Israel, and he said in the sight of Israel, Sun, stand thou still upon Gibeon; and thou, Moon, in the valley of Ajalon.", insight:"He spoke to the sun as a servant speaks to a fellow servant; bold prayer treats nature as subject to its Maker."},
  {q:"How long did the sun stand still and the moon stay?", options:["Until the people had avenged themselves upon their enemies","For one hour","Until the next morning","For forty days"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 10:13",verse:"And the sun stood still, and the moon stayed, until the people had avenged themselves upon their enemies. Is not this written in the book of Jasher? So the sun stood still in the midst of heaven, and hasted not to go down about a whole day.", insight:"The sun waited on Israel's battle; time itself paused so that God's justice could finish its work."},
  {q:"What is said of that day?", options:["There was no day like that before it or after it, that the LORD hearkened unto the voice of a man","It was the longest night in Israel","The stars fell from heaven","The Jordan flowed backward"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 10:14",verse:"And there was no day like that before it or after it, that the LORD hearkened unto the voice of a man: for the LORD fought for Israel.", insight:"No day like it, before or after; God broke the pattern of all history to answer one man's prayer."},
  {q:"Where did the five kings flee and hide?", options:["In a cave at Makkedah","In the forests of Hebron","In the king's palace","Across the Jordan"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 10:16",verse:"But these five kings fled, and hid themselves in a cave at Makkedah.", insight:"Five kings in a cave: the mighty who trusted in walls and horses ended hiding in a hole in the ground."},
  {q:"What did Joshua command to be done at the cave's mouth?", options:["Roll great stones upon the mouth of the cave, and set men by it","Burn the cave with fire","Fill the cave with water","Seal it with the king's ring"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 10:18",verse:"And Joshua said, Roll great stones upon the mouth of the cave, and set men by it for to keep them:", insight:"Great stones at the mouth, and men to keep them; Joshua finished the battle first and saved the kings for its proper end."},
  {q:"What did Joshua's captains do to the five kings?", options:["They put their feet upon the necks of the kings","They bound them in fetters of brass","They set crowns upon their heads","They anointed them with oil"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 10:24",verse:"And it came to pass, when they brought out those kings unto Joshua, that Joshua called for all the men of Israel, and said unto the captains of the men of war which went with him, Come near, put your feet upon the necks of these kings. And they came near, and put their feet upon the necks of them.", insight:"Feet on the necks of kings: God let His captains taste the victory with their own feet, so they would never forget it."},
  {q:"What charge did Joshua give his people over the fallen kings?", options:["Fear not, nor be dismayed, be strong and of good courage","Take their crowns for yourselves","Spare the youngest king","Return to Gilgal at once"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 10:25",verse:"And Joshua said unto them, Fear not, nor be dismayed, be strong and of good courage: for thus shall the LORD do to all your enemies against whom ye fight.", insight:"Over conquered kings he preached courage, not cruelty; victory is a pulpit for faith, not an excuse for pride."},
  {q:"What was done with the five kings?", options:["Joshua smote them, and slew them, and hanged them on five trees until the evening","They were made servants in Jerusalem","They were ransomed for gold","They were exiled to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 10:26",verse:"And afterward Joshua smote them, and slew them, and hanged them on five trees: and they were hanging upon the trees until the evening.", insight:"Five kings on five trees till evening; God writes the end of human pride in a script every nation can read."},
  {q:"Why did Joshua take all those kings and their land at one time?", options:["Because the LORD God of Israel fought for Israel","Because his army was the largest","Because the kings were divided","Because of his own wisdom"], a:0, ref:"Joshua 10:42",verse:"And all these kings and their land did Joshua take at one time, because the LORD God of Israel fought for Israel.", insight:"The secret of the whole campaign in one line: the LORD fought for Israel. That is still the only explanation that matters."}
 ]}
]);
